<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jorge Icaza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jorge Icaza was an Ecuadorian novelist and playwright, a leading writer of the indigenista movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American literature. His novel Huasipungo of 1934 described the brutal exploitation of indigenous labourers on highland estates and was translated into many languages. He later served as director of the National Library of Ecuador and as ambassador.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jorge-icaza.jpg" alt="Jorge Icaza" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Firma de Jorge Icaza · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firma_de_Jorge_Icaza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
